      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A parallelogram is sometimes a rectangle.\n\nII. A kite is never a quadrilateral.\n\nIII. A square is always a trapezoid.",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191275/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A parallelogram is a rectangle when all the angles are right.\n\n- Statement II is false. A kite is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A trapezoid has only $1$ pair of opposite sides that are parallel. However, in a square, $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel, and so doesn't fit the definition of a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true.",
