      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191275/assets/e3708d7723606224e201147b432250dd759022fd7ed275dabebf51935d68d8f8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rhombus?",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191275/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. All its sides are congruent and the opposite sides are parallel, so it is a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a trapezoid. Since it has only one pair of parallel sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid.  Since it has only one pair of parallel sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\nTherefore, shape $Q$ is a rhombus. The shapes $P,$ $R,$ and $S$ are not rhombuses.",
